+# Add member session revocation
+
+Status: accepted. Partially supersedes [Use signed cookies for sessions](0002-use-signed-cookies-for-sessions.md).
+
+## Context
+
+A member's phone was stolen while logged in. A volunteer must be able to end that member's access immediately.
+The signed cookie design deliberately accepted the absence of individual revocation; expiry and client cookie
+deletion cannot meet this new requirement. Rotating the shared key would log out every member and is restricted
+to between pickups. Process-local revocation would fail across servers and deploys.
+
+## Decision
+
+Retain signed cookies and add a version per revoked member in the existing Postgres database. Signing includes the
+current version; verification compares it with the database. Logout atomically increments that member's version.
+Missing rows and legacy cookies use version zero, preserving existing sessions during the upgrade while allowing
+them to be revoked. A fresh login uses the new version. All existing cookies for a member are revoked together.
+
+Use Postgres rather than another service or per-session records. Revocation timestamps would require issuance
+timestamps and careful clock ordering across servers; integer versions avoid that dependence.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Logout survives deploys and takes effect on all upgraded servers after commit. The database stores at most one row
+per member who has been logged out, without accumulating expired sessions or needing a cleanup job. Version rows
+must persist to prevent revoked cookies becoming valid again.
+
+Signing and verification now require database reads and availability. Database errors propagate without granting
+access or reporting a failed logout as successful. This intentionally changes the earlier decision's no-query
+tradeoff to support immediate revocation. Deployment must apply the migration and upgrade every server before
+logout is used. Current behavior and deployment instructions belong in [sessions](../features/sessions.md).
