+An authorized volunteer can call `logout(member_id)` to invalidate all existing cookies for that member immediately
+after the database transaction commits, including a stolen phone's cookie. Other members stay logged in. The member
+can log in again and receive a cookie with the new version. Request renewal must follow successful verification;
+`sign` is a trusted issuance function, not an authentication check. Authorization for volunteer actions belongs in
+the calling application.
+
+Versions live in `member_session_versions` in the existing shared Postgres database, with at most one row per member
+who has been logged out. Updates increment the version atomically, including concurrent logouts. Never delete or
+reset these rows: doing so could make revoked cookies valid again. There are no per-session rows or cleanup jobs.
+Database errors propagate; logout must not be reported as successful when the write fails, and authentication must
+not bypass version checks during an outage.
+
+Before deploying, install `requirements.txt`, apply `migrations/0001_member_session_versions.sql` to the shared
+database, and set `DATABASE_URL` for every web server. All servers must run the version-checking code before
+volunteers rely on logout; an old server still accepts revoked cookies. Keep the signing key unchanged during this
+upgrade. Rolling back to code without version checks would restore access to revoked cookies until they expire.
+
+Rotating the shared key still invalidates every session cookie and logs everyone out, so rotate it only between
+pickups. Ensure all web servers load the new key together.
 
